      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Assess the need for folate testing, frequency of corrective action, and determine reference level for serum folate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Serum folate levels in 5313 samples from 4448 patients, and clinical data were reviewed for patient characteristics and for (a) evidence of corrective action in patients with serum folate values <5.5 ng/mL, and (b) differences in patients with serum folate levels <5.5 ng/mL and patients with levels >25.7 ng/mL."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The prevalence of serum folate levels, in patients, <3.0, <4.0, <5.5, <7.0 and <13.0 ng/mL was 0.58%, 1.55%, 4.9%, 9.98% and 43.21% respectively. Patients with serum folate levels <5.5 ng/mL had lower serum albumin and hemoglobin. In 64% of patients with serum folate >25.7 ng/mL the sample was collected after supplementation with folic acid. Of the 128 patients with serum folate <5.5 ng/mL documentation of supplementation was present in only 38.9%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Serum folate levels are below the current \"normal\" level of 3.0 ng/mL in a larger proportion of tertiary care patients than that reported for ambulatory patients. In patients with folate deficiency, corrective action is lacking in >60% of the patients. Since serum folate levels ≥13.0 ng/mL are needed for optimal prevention of neural tube defects in the embryo/fetus, we propose that normal serum folate level should be designated to be ≥13.0 ng/mL."
        }
      ]},
